<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of David</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Davidic line</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> was the dynasty that, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, descended from King David and ruled the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> for about four centuries, until the Babylonian conquest of 587/586 BCE. The phrase appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a> of the 9th century BCE, its earliest known mention outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. Jewish and Christian tradition expected the Messiah to come from David's line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-david.jpg" alt="House of David" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Star_of_David.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the biblical narrative, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> of the Tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> fought a protracted conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ish-bosheth">Ish-bosheth</a> of the Tribe of Benjamin, who had succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a>. The prophet Samuel had anointed David as the true king. After Ish-bosheth was assassinated by his own army captains, David is traditionally believed to have taken the throne around 1010 BCE. He ruled first over Judah alone from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, and after seven and a half years the other tribes, leaderless, chose him as well. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, whose mother was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bathsheba">Bathsheba</a>.</p><p>Little is conclusively known about the house outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. The Tel Dan Stele, dated to about 840 BCE, mentions the death of a reigning king from what is interpreted as the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, and it is the only explicit mention of David outside the Bible. Some scholars, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein">Israel Finkelstein</a> and Neil Silberman, think David and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> probably existed but were chieftains of a modest society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> rather than rulers of a wealthy empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s death the ten northern tribes rejected his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rehoboam">Rehoboam</a> and chose Jeroboam as king, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> in the north, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and Benjamin kept Rehoboam and formed the Kingdom of Judah in the south. Apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>, only the Judahite monarchs belonged to the Davidic line.</p><p>The earliest unambiguously attested king of the line is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzziah">Uzziah</a>, in the 8th century BCE. After him each king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> is attested in some form, except Amon. Ahaz's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> appears on royal seals and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s Annals. Jeconiah is named in Babylonian documents listing the rations given to him and his sons as prisoners. The Kingdom of Judah fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> after the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple was destroyed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a> later founded an independent Jewish kingdom, but it was not considered connected to the Davidic line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later rabbinical authorities granted the office of exilarch, head of Babylonian Jewry, to families tracing male-line descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. The office continued after the Arab conquest in the 7th century and exilarchs were appointed until the 11th century.</p><p>In Jewish eschatology the Messiah is expected to be a king whose paternal bloodline traces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, who will rule during the Messianic Age. Christians hold that the line continued to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, hence the title Son of David, according to the genealogies in Matthew and Luke; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> do not accept this. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> mentions the family of David once.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Davidic line” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army was the decisive power broker, and most changes of dynasty were military coups, whereas Judah kept the <b>House of David</b> on its throne throughout.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the books of Kings, the northern tribes rejected Solomon’s son Rehoboam at Shechem and made Jeroboam their king, founding the Kingdom of Israel, while the tribe of Judah, with much of Benjamin, stayed loyal to Rehoboam and the <b>House of David</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest reference to the “<b>House of David</b>” outside the Bible appears on the Tel Dan stele, an Aramaic inscription found in northern Israel in 1993.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
